  * Back button hijacking is when a site interferes with user browser navigation by manipulating the browser history or other functionalities, preventing them from using their back button to immediately get back to the page they came from. 
 
+### Back button hijacking FAQ
+
+#### Some back button hijacking was caused by third-party advertising platforms. Are you preventing sites from importing code hosted by advertising platforms?
+
+No, site owners can continue to work with advertising platforms and import code that they host. We only take action on pages that exhibit back button hijacking behavior.
+
+#### Can users report sites that engage in back button hijacking?
+
+Yes, users can report potential spammy behavior through the spam submission report.
+
+#### Does this policy apply to sites in Google Discover?
+
+Yes, Discover is a part of Google Search and our spam policies apply to Discover as well.
+
+#### Does the back button hijacking policy apply if a user arrives at a website from a non-Search channel (such as social media) and experiences back button hijacking when trying to return to the source?
+
+If back button hijacking happens when a user is browsing within a browser, then the policy applies.
+
+#### Does the back button hijacking policy apply when a website opens an external app, which then prevents the user from returning to the original destination?
+
+No, app navigation and "open in app" behaviors are outside the scope of the back button hijacking policy.
+
